>
  <article class="paper">
    <div class="wrap article">
      <header class="article__head">
        <h1>EICR for Leeds landlords: the 2026 rules, costs and fines</h1>
        <p class="article__meta">
          By {client.owner}, electrician, ES Elec · July 2026 <!-- TEMP author -->
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="article__body">
        <p>
          Every rented home in England must have a satisfactory EICR renewed
          at least every five years, under the Electrical Safety Standards in
          the Private Rented Sector (England) Regulations 2020. In Leeds in
          2026 the inspection costs £140 to £240 for a typical house, and
          the council can fine up to £30,000 per breach for not having
          one. <!-- TEMP prices -->
        </p>

        <h2>The five rules that bite</h2>
        <p>
          One: renew at least every five years, or sooner if the last report
          says so. Two: new tenants get a copy before they move in, existing
          tenants within 28 days of the inspection. Three: the council gets a
          copy within 7 days if they ask for one. Four: C1 and C2 fixes done
          within 28 days, or faster if the report says, with written
          confirmation to tenant and council. Five: keep the paper trail. The
          fine is per breach, and "I emailed the agent" is not a paper trail.
        </p>

        <h2>What it costs in Leeds in 2026</h2>
        <table class="costs">
          <caption>
            Typical EICR prices, Leeds, 2026. Movers: circuit count, access,
            multiple boards. <!-- TEMP figures -->
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Property</th><th scope="col">Price</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>1-2 bed flat</strong></td><td>£140</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>3-bed semi</strong></td><td>£160 to £190</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>4-bed and up</strong></td><td>£200 to £240</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>HMOs</strong></td><td>priced on circuits</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2>Reading the report without panicking</h2>
        <p>
          C1 means danger now, and it gets made safe before the electrician
          leaves. C2 means potentially dangerous, 28 days to fix on a rental.
          C3 means improvement recommended and it is STILL A PASS. FI means
          further investigation, and the report stays unsatisfactory until
          it's resolved. The bit agents miss: a C3-only report needs nothing
          doing. Don't be upsold on a pass.
        </p>

        <h2>The Leeds-specific bit</h2>
        <p>
          Student HMOs in Headingley and Hyde Park carry more circuits and
          more wear than the same house owner-occupied. Book the EICR for
          daytime between tenancies if you can: empty house, full access,
          faster test, lower price.
        </p>

        <div class="band">
          <h2 style="font-size: 1.4rem;">Renewal date coming up?</h2>
          <p style="color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--t-small);">
            Two or more properties booked together get priced together.
            Report back the same evening, plain English. <!-- TEMP promise -->
          </p>
          <div class="acts">
            <a class="btn btn--call" href={`tel:${tel}`}>Call Eddie</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
